    "case_id": "600",
    "case_summary": "A 65-year-old man with a history of hepatitis B, hepatitis C, rheumatoid arthritis, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, hypertension, hyperlipidemia, and obesity presented with several months of facial swelling, dysphagia, and dysarthria. Shortly thereafter, he developed a pruritic rash on his trunk and upper extremities. He did not report a personal or family history of angioedema. His medications included lisinopril, atorvastatin, albuterol, and ibuprofen for rheumatoid arthritis. The patient had discontinued etanercept six months before presentation. He presented to the emergency department two weeks after seeing his primary care provider for a persistent periorbital, chest, and upper extremity rash. His rash worsened, and he noted new throat swelling and weakness.\n\nVital signs were stable on arrival. Physical examination was notable for anasarca and a diffuse coalescent erythematous rash over the extremities, chest, and abdomen, with scaling and crusting of the back. There was a heliotrope rash and erythematous lip swelling. He had weakness to resistance in all extremities and a muffled voice without tongue swelling. Over several days, the patient's shoulder and hip weakness progressed, and he could not lift his arms above his head or stand without assistance.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    - Basic metabolic panel and complete blood count were unremarkable    - Elevated C-reactive protein and erythrocyte sedimentation rate    - Negative viral respiratory panel    - C4, C1q, and C1 esterase inhibitor were unremarkable    - Normal serum and urine protein electrophoresis    - Elevated transaminases    - Elevated anti-Sjögren syndrome–related antigen A autoantibodies    - High antinuclear antibody titers    - Negative anti-Jo antibody    - Elevated creatine kinase    - Positive myositis panel for anti-SAE antibodies  - Imaging Studies:    - CT neck: Mild submental inflammatory stranding with no discrete fluid collection or abscess    - Liver ultrasound: Hepatic steatosis    - CT chest: Multiple nodular and airspace opacities, including right lower lobe nodule  - Pathology:    - Skin biopsy results were consistent with Wong-type presentation",
